## Why This Is Asked

Tests crash‑consistency and lock‑free design under PMEM constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Crash‑consistency with persistent memory using CAS, fences, and transactional updates
- Per‑thread caches with a central allocator to reduce cross‑socket traffic
- Recovery and durability: reconstruct free lists from persistent root and/or logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: pmem_alloc
function pmem_alloc() {
  if (!threadCache.empty()) return threadCache.pop();
  let batch = centralPool.acquireBatch();
  threadCache.fill(batch);
  return threadCache.pop();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect fragmentation in this design?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded contexts with minimal locking?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and practical reuse of barriers without locks.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic fetch_add and compare_exchange
- Memory_order_acquire/release or relaxed with correct ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier for reuse
- Spin-wait costs and NUMA locality

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_int count; atomic_int sense; int N; } barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int N){
  atomic_init(&b->count, 0);
  atomic_init(&b->sense, 0);
  b->N = N;
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int *local_sense){
  int my_sense = !*local_sense;
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) + 1 == b->N){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_xor_explicit(&b->sense, 1, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) != *local_sense){
      // spin
    }
  }
  *local_sense = my_sense;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic N? 
- How to avoid energy waste on long waits?

